menu "breadboard: bb_mqtt_client"

config BB_MQTT_CLIENT_KEEPALIVE
    int "MQTT keepalive interval in seconds"
    default 120
    range 10 3600
    help
        MQTT keepalive sent to the broker. If the broker closes the connection
        after this many seconds of inactivity, esp-mqtt will reconnect
        automatically (unless disable_auto_reconnect is set).
        Note: CONFIG_MQTT_TASK_STACK_SIZE may need to be raised to 6144 or
        higher when TLS is enabled (mbedTLS handshake requires ~5-8 KB stack).

config BB_MQTT_CLIENT_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "MQTT network timeout (ms)"
    default 30000
    range 1000 120000
    help
        Maps to esp_mqtt_client_config_t.network.timeout_ms. esp-mqtt
        defaults to 10000, which aborts writes ("write didn't complete in
        specified timeout") on a marginal link and forces a reconnect.
        30s tolerates a slow/weak link without churning.

config BB_MQTT_CLIENT_RX_BUFFER_BYTES
    int "MQTT receive buffer size (bytes)"
    default 1024
    range 64 4096
    help
        Receive buffer for the bb_mqtt_client component (maps to
        esp_mqtt_client_config_t.buffer.size), overriding the global
        CONFIG_MQTT_BUFFER_SIZE. ALSO sizes the app-level reassembly buffer
        used by bb_mqtt_client_on_message() (B1-487) to accumulate a message across
        multiple MQTT_EVENT_DATA fragments — a message whose total size
        exceeds this value is dropped (logged) rather than delivered.
        Default 1024 covers real meta/stats-shaped subscribe payloads
        (the 256-byte legacy default only fit publish-only/non-subscribing
        clients whose RX only ever sees short protocol ACK frames); raise it
        further if your client subscribes to topics with larger payloads.

config BB_MQTT_CLIENT_TX_BUFFER_BYTES
    int "MQTT transmit buffer size (bytes)"
    default 1024
    range 128 4096
    help
        Transmit buffer (maps to esp_mqtt_client_config_t.buffer.out_size);
        1024 fits typical JSON telemetry.

config BB_MQTT_CLIENT_OUTBOX_LIMIT_BYTES
    int "MQTT outbox size limit (bytes)"
    default 4096
    range 0 65536
    help
        Maps to esp_mqtt_client_config_t.outbox.limit. esp-mqtt's outbox
        holds queued/unacknowledged messages (including bb_mqtt_client_enqueue()
        traffic) and is unbounded by default (0 = no limit), which risks
        unbounded heap growth on no-PSRAM targets under sustained publish
        pressure. A non-zero limit makes the header's documented
        BB_ERR_NO_SPACE retry contract (see bb_mqtt_client_enqueue()) actually
        reachable: esp_mqtt_client_enqueue() returns -2 (mapped to
        BB_ERR_NO_SPACE) once the outbox would exceed this many bytes.

        This is ONE client-wide limit, not enqueue()-only: esp-mqtt applies
        the SAME check to bb_mqtt_client_publish() calls with qos>0 (qos=0
        publishes are fire-and-forget and never enter the outbox). Existing
        qos>0 publish() callers therefore also newly see BB_ERR_NO_SPACE
        back-pressure under this default, not just enqueue() callers.

        Set to 0 to restore esp-mqtt's unbounded default.

config BB_MQTT_CLIENT_SUSPEND_STOP_ONLY
    bool "Suspend uses stop-only (keeps client resident, no destroy/recreate)"
    default n
    help
        When y, bb_mqtt_client_suspend_default() calls esp_mqtt_client_stop() only
        (keeps client handle, buffers, and task resident, ~11 KB -- plus up
        to BB_MQTT_CLIENT_OUTBOX_LIMIT_BYTES more if the outbox is occupied
        at suspend time) and bb_mqtt_client_resume_default() calls
        esp_mqtt_client_start() only — no destroy, no realloc, no NVS reload.

        ONLY safe when bb_mem_arena_tls covers the TLS handshake that follows
        the suspend window.  The ~11 KB (+ outbox) residency means the
        largest_free_block improvement is smaller than the full destroy
        path; use this mode only when the arena already reserves enough
        headroom.

        Default n = full destroy+recreate (original behavior): suspend calls
        bb_mqtt_client_stop() which destroys the client and frees all buffers; resume
        calls auto_client_create_from_nvs() to recreate.

endmenu
